 fairly clear  that these islands are a power are the  Spratlys and not the Paracels and to cut  a long story short and I have quite a  long story here it comes down to a  meeting of the ROC s Military Committee  in the 1st of September a secret meeting  at which they decide that they have no  claim that China has no claim to the  Spratly Islands and they're agitated  about it they say we know our fishermen  go there but we have  no evidence that we've ever made a claim  and this is quite different from the the  southwestern authorities who I think are  unaware of this meeting of the military  committee and continue to agitate about  the fate of the Paracels and continue to  mix the idea that what the French have  annexed is in the Paracels and they say  they continue to be confused about  exactly that so and the Southwest  province used this issue as a means to  attack the national nationalist  legitimacy of the government and Nanjing  have already been accused of being too  soft on the Japanese of selling out the  country and that they are the pure you  know hardline representatives of proper  China and they use this issue to attack  to attack Nanjing so it's in their  interests to keep stoking up the claim  and they deliberately deliberately or  unwittingly confuse Paracels and  Spratlys  at this point and what happens really is  that aren't shortly after this a series  of articles are printed in Chinese  newspapers trying to explain the history  of the South China Sea Islands not to  foreigners not to sort of assert a claim  to the French or the British or the  Japanese or anybody but to their own  people so this is what we talk about  many of the construction of the G of  what he trying to generate a narrative  in which Chinese people can understand  that they have always owned these  islands and always always been the  rightful owners by picking out  references going back to 1928 going back  to the the Admirals expedition of 1909  and he he gives an interview in August  1983 talking about his his old his old  voyage and Jenner and helps to generate  a national narrative you like about the  connection between the islands and the  mainland and what's fascinating to me  and I came to this the other way because  I was trying to trace the references  which are being used in English now and  where those sources and references come  from and it's clear to me that the first  very first english-language writing  about the South China Sea dispute only  happens in 1975 after the battle the  Paracels the previous year and they're  written by in the  international lawyers who usually have a  background in China so paper by hunger  Chu and Chandra Park - was from Taiwan  but nothing but tau Chang he was here at  the LLM at Peking University and they  are using their original sources are  these newspapers and magazines which are  published in August September October of  1933 and I could list them if you want  but there are about you know six  articles which define the Chinese  narrative and it's connection to the  South China Sea Islands and they are  then picked up and quoted and we quoted  for the next 80 or 90 years before they  are then last for so 40 years to 1775  they continued to be quoted in Chinese  and they have now performed the  foundation of a lots of the English  language writing about the South China  Sea dispute and that narrative is the  narrative you find in Marwan Samuels  book on the South China Sea disputes  it's the narrative you find in mark  Valencia's writing about the search  honesty disputes and it all comes back  to a few badly informed newspaper  articles published in the summer of 1933
 

